                            <a name="comment221"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: The man who walked away
</h4>

<p>
Me like. Especially this:<br />
<br />
"But the language of bookkeeping leaves no room for intimacies or nicknames, for neighbors or blocks, for things everybody saw, for airplanes falling out of the sky."<br />
<br />
It sounds like what do, or can do, but don't always. It's the differnce between an article and a story. Which would you rather read?<br />
<br />
But, about this Brick gem, there's also this: A friend of mine in NYC read it and hated it. Said it was a story that said nothing about anything. Said nothing about Park Slope or plane crashes or New York City. It was, to this friend, a story of no significance, and had no place in a newspaper. I think the word "elitist" was thrown around a bit. The friend - who reads newspapers as a city-dwelling newspaper reader, not a journalist - said it was "writing for writers." <br />
 <br />
Kruse, you wanna blow up on this one buddy?
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		ramsey	

 at November 19,2005 16:59
</div>
